<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Boniface</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 675–754</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Boniface of Mainz, Winfrid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Boniface was an English monk, born Winfrid, who led the papal mission to the Germans. He worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, organised the dioceses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a>, Regensburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a>, and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> and the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a>. As archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> he reformed the Frankish church. He was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> in 754 and buried at Fulda.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-boniface.jpg" alt="Saint Boniface" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cornelis Bloemaert · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Boniface_by_Cornelis_Bloemaert.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Saint Boniface</b> reorganised its church, founding the bishopric of Würzburg in 742.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-thuringia">Duchy of Thuringia</a></li><li><blockquote>The see of Mainz was raised to an archbishopric around 780 to 782, building on the work of <b>Saint Boniface</b>, archbishop there from 746.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-mainz">Electorate of Mainz</a></li><li><blockquote>Sturm, a disciple of <b>Saint Boniface</b>, founded the monastery at Fulda in 744, and Boniface was buried there in 754.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-abbey-of-fulda">Prince-Abbey of Fulda</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Saint Boniface</b> made his kinsman Willibald the first bishop of Eichstätt in the 740s.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-eichstatt">Prince-Bishopric of Eichstätt</a></li><li><blockquote>The diocese goes back to Saint Corbinian, who worked at Freising around 720, and was formally organised by <b>Saint Boniface</b> in 739.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-freising">Prince-Bishopric of Freising</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Saint Boniface</b> organised the diocese of Passau in 739.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-passau">Prince-Bishopric of Passau</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Crediton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Dokkum, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.79,-3.65&amp;year=726" aria-label="Crediton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4467 401 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Saint Boniface"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4917 671h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4917 671h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.79,-3.65&amp;year=726">What was here in 726 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
